course: euphemisms
lesson: 7
title: Soft words for hard things: the toilet and bodily functions
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Our bodies do messy things: they make gas, sweat, get stuck, run, throw up, and every month many women have a period.
explain: People feel embarrassed to say these plainly, so they use soft or funny words instead.
explain: Polite words like "perspire" or "an upset stomach" sound clean; jokes like "Delhi belly" or "technicolour yawn" laugh the awkwardness away.
explain: Vivid example: a sign in a shop says "feminine hygiene products" — it simply means pads and tampons, but the long official name sounds less personal.
explain: Knowing these words helps you understand what is really happening — for example that a friend "had an accident" does not mean a car crash.
fact: "Pass gas" is a polite American way to say fart.
fact: "Toot" is a childish, joking word for a fart.
fact: "Let one rip" is slang for fart loudly.
fact: "Glow" is an old British joke word for sweat when said about ladies: "Horses sweat, men perspire, ladies glow."
fact: "Perspire" is the polite word for sweat.
fact: "Irregular" is a polite way to say constipated, when poo will not come out easily.
fact: "The runs" is slang for diarrhoea.
fact: "Delhi belly" is a joking British name for diarrhoea caught while travelling.
fact: "Montezuma's revenge" is a joking American name for diarrhoea caught while travelling, especially in Mexico.
fact: "An upset stomach" is a polite way to say diarrhoea or being sick.
fact: "Had an accident" often means someone wet or soiled themselves, not a crash.
fact: "Be sick" in British English means vomit, not just feel ill.
fact: "Throw up" is the everyday word for vomit.
fact: "Technicolour yawn" is joking Australian slang for vomit.
fact: "Chunder" is Australian slang for vomit.
fact: "That time of the month" means a woman's monthly period.
fact: "Aunt Flo" is a joking American way to talk about a period; there is no aunt.
fact: "Feminine hygiene products" is the official name for sanitary pads and tampons.
fact: "Protective underwear" is the official name for adult nappies worn by people who cannot hold their wee.
fact: "Go potty" means use the toilet, said to young children, mostly in the US.
exercise: What does "pass gas" mean? | Fart | Fill up the car | Pass | Overtake a truck
exercise: A little boy giggles "I tooted!" What did he do? | Farted | Honked | Played a trumpet | Beeped a bike bell
exercise: What does "let one rip" mean? | Fart loudly | Tear a page | Throw a ball hard | Shout loudly
exercise: In the old saying "ladies glow", what does "glow" mean? | Sweat | Shine with happiness | Blush | Wear make-up
exercise: What does "perspire" mean? | Sweat | Pant | Feel inspired | Get tired
exercise: An advert says "Feeling irregular? Try more fibre." What problem is it about? | Constipation | Sleeping at odd times | A messy timetable | Loneliness
exercise: What does it mean to have "the runs"? | To have diarrhoea | To run fast | To be late all the time | To have a running nose
exercise: What is "Delhi belly"? | Diarrhoea caught while travelling | A spicy Indian dish | A big tummy | A cooking show
exercise: What is "Montezuma's revenge"? | Diarrhoea caught while travelling, especially in Mexico | A famous old battle | A Mexican board game | A spicy sauce
exercise: The school nurse says "She went home with an upset stomach." What most likely happened? | She had diarrhoea or was sick | She was sad after a fight | She ate too little lunch | She was angry at a teacher
exercise: The nursery teacher says "Leo had an accident today." What most likely happened? | He wet or soiled himself | He was hit by a car | He broke a toy | He fell off a chair
exercise: A British child says "I was sick on the bus." What happened? | She vomited | She got a cold | She felt a bit tired | She had a fever
exercise: What does "throw up" mean? | Vomit | Throw a ball high | Wake | Jump in the air
exercise: An Aussie says "He did a technicolour yawn." What did he do? | Vomited | Yawned | Watched a colour TV | Painted a picture
exercise: What does "chunder" mean in Australia? | Vomit | Thunder | Chew loudly | Sleep heavily
exercise: A woman says "It's that time of the month." What does she mean? | She has her period | Her rent is due | It is pay day | It is a full moon
exercise: A teenager says "Aunt Flo is visiting." What does she mean? | She has her period | Her aunt has come to stay | A relative is ill | She is going on holiday
exercise: What are "feminine hygiene products"? | Sanitary pads and tampons | Women's shampoos | Perfumes for girls | Soaps for babies
exercise: What is "protective underwear" in a chemist's shop? | Adult nappies | Armoured clothing | Thermal vests | Swimming costumes
exercise: An American mom asks her toddler "Do you need to go potty?" What is she asking? | If he needs the toilet | If he wants to plant flowers | If he wants to play | If he is hungry
test: Holiday postcard: "Great trip, but Dad got Delhi belly on day two." What happened to Dad? | He got diarrhoea | He ate too much curry and got fat | He got sunburnt | He lost his luggage
test: "ugh someone let one rip in the lift 🤢" — what happened in the lift? | Someone farted loudly | Someone tore their trousers | Someone ripped a poster | Someone shouted
test: The coach yells "stop moanin, you're not sweatin, you're just glowin!" What is the coach joking about? | Their sweat | Their bright T-shirts | Their red faces from shame | Sunburn
test: A report from summer camp: "Two campers had the runs after the barbecue." What was wrong? | They had diarrhoea | They ran a race | They ran away | They were late for dinner
test: Mate: "big night, he chundered all over the footpath." What did he do? | He vomited | He fell over | He shouted | He tripped on a crack
test: A note to the teacher: "Sam may be irregular this week due to his new medicine." What does it mean? | Sam may be constipated | Sam may come to school late | Sam may miss some lessons | Sam may behave strangely
test: In a Mexico travel guide: "Drink bottled water to avoid Montezuma's revenge." What are tourists avoiding? | Getting diarrhoea | Being robbed | An angry king | Sunstroke
test: Grandma whispers "Poor Grandpa had a little accident on the way home." Was there a crash? | No, he wet himself | Yes, a small car crash | Yes, he fell off his bike | No, he got lost
test: "omg I'm gonna throw up, this ride's mental" — how does the rider feel? | Like vomiting | Like throwing a ball | Like jumping off | Like shouting for joy
test: A shop sign says "Feminine hygiene products – aisle 4." What will you find in aisle 4? | Pads and tampons | Women's clothes | Make-up | Cleaning sprays for kitchens
test: Leo reckons "perspire" means to feel inspired. Is he right? | No, it means sweat | Yes, it means feel inspired | Yes, it means breathe in | No, it means sulk
test: "Mum, Lily tooted at the table! Gross!" What did Lily do? | She farted | She blew a whistle | She burped | She sang loudly
test: A care-home list: "Order more protective underwear for Room 12." What is needed? | Adult nappies | Warm pyjamas | Safety helmets | Socks that do not slip
exam: Identify the principal meaning of the expression "to pass gas" in contemporary American usage. | To expel gas from the body; to fart | To transfer a gas cylinder to another person | To overtake a vehicle on the road | To refuel a motor vehicle
exam: It is reported that a pupil "was sick in the corridor" in British usage. Ascertain what occurred. | The pupil vomited | The pupil was found to have a fever | The pupil felt unwell | The pupil was absent through illness
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the colloquial Australian expression "technicolour yawn". | An act of vomiting | A prolonged yawn caused by fatigue | A television broadcast in colour | A painting of vivid hue
exam: Given that a pharmacy catalogue lists "protective underwear", determine which of the following most accurately characterises the product. | Adult nappies for incontinence | Garments offering protection against cold | Clothing worn for contact sports | Underwear treated against insects
